{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>קרבה</b> — Rabbi Moses said: for it is קרבה, like וקרב אותם." "And the word גאלה — like גאולתה. And thus Rabbi Moses interprets: קרבה, by way of an imperative of a transitive verb, and he prays: 'Draw near, redeem my soul,' like וקרב אותם, which is imperative, like בלע ה' פלג לשונם. Therefore the vav of וקרב is fully open [as a kamatz gadol] and גאלה has a patach, like זהבה שללה; therefore it is גאולתה. Thus the sage, may he live, interpreted to me. But the correct interpretation: that it is like זכרה לי." "<b>וגאלה</b> — an imperative, on the pattern of ולמדה את בני ישראל, or the word אשר is elided. And so it is: קרבה אל נפשי אשר גאלה. And the meaning: the one who redeems it. And the meaning of למען אויבי: that they do violence to me."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 69, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra's argument turns on vowel distinctions: kamatz gadol (full vowel) in וקרב marks it as imperative; patach in גאלה (instead of shva) parallels forms like זהבה, indicating a possessive suffix. The cryptic reference to זכרה לי as 'the correct interpretation' is left untranslated in its logic."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:33:30.823261557Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}